WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:07.360
Speaker 1: So we spoke yesterday about the
sort of disturbing reality that at this late

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:16.440
stage in the game, you still see most prominent
Republicans quite fervently, feverishly and

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:21.560
feverishly behind Donald Trump. We at this
rally in Iowa over the weekend, Chuck Grassley

00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:26.620
was there saying, Trump's great all this different
stuff. It's very, very concerning. And one

00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:34.129
of the ways in which Republicans signal there,
at least for now, continued public loyalty

00:00:34.129 --> 00:00:37.879
to Trump, whatever they believe privately,
they may hate the guy privately. Think he's

00:00:37.879 --> 00:00:42.109
terrible for the Republican Party, whatever,
right? But the way you signal that at least

00:00:42.109 --> 00:00:48.519
publicly you're still behind Trump is when
you're asked, Did Donald Trump have the election

00:00:48.519 --> 00:00:56.300
stolen from him? You don't say no. You come
up with anything you can to avoid saying no.

00:00:56.300 --> 00:01:03.190
And that's exactly what radical bomb throwing
Republican Congressman Steve Scalise did over

00:01:03.190 --> 00:01:07.290
the weekend when he was interviewed by Chris
Wallace. Now it took me a couple of days to

00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:10.600
get to this. We were off Monday for the federal
holiday and I didn't have time yesterday,

00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:15.490
but I want to get to it today. It happened
on Sunday. Chris Wallace just keeps asking

00:01:15.490 --> 00:01:20.939
Steve Scalise, was the election stolen from
Donald Trump or will you say that it was not

00:01:20.939 --> 00:01:27.329
that that Joe Biden simply won? And Steve
Scalise goes through every single iteration

00:01:27.329 --> 00:01:32.170
of how you don't say no. So let's just jump
into it. We'll go through these examples and

00:01:32.170 --> 00:01:37.439
we'll talk about them, and it's interesting
to see how Chris Wallace handles it. But really,

00:01:37.439 --> 00:01:43.649
for me, the more interesting part is to analyze
the language that Scalise uses to answer questions

00:01:43.649 --> 00:01:49.850
that are the ones being asked in order to
avoid simply saying it wasn't stolen from

00:01:49.850 --> 00:01:54.380
Donald Trump. Let's jump in U.S. capital.
Speaker 2: Let me ask you one last question

00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:58.780
in this regard. Clearly, there were irregularities
in the last election. There are irregularities

00:01:58.780 --> 00:02:03.990
in all elections. But I I want to ask you
a specific question do you think the twenty

00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:09.979
twenty election was stolen from Donald Trump
Speaker 1: and to be I am not going to just

00:02:09.979 --> 00:02:15.350
I'm not going to try not to jump in all the
time. The way I've been saying this is anecdotal

00:02:15.350 --> 00:02:21.140
irregularities aside, which there would always
be in an election in a country of 330 million

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:28.780
people. Do we have any evidence that the irregularities
in even one state rose to the level of being

00:02:28.780 --> 00:02:33.530
materially substantive as far as who the winner
was? That's that's the core

00:02:33.530 --> 00:02:39.710
Speaker 2: and continued continuing to make
that charge. Not having states do election

00:02:39.710 --> 00:02:45.230
reforms, but specifically making this charge
that the election was stolen. Do you think

00:02:45.230 --> 00:02:51.590
that that hurts undermines American democracy?
Well, Chris, I've been very clear from the

00:02:51.590 --> 00:02:55.840
beginning. If you look at a number of states,
they didn't follow their state passed laws

00:02:55.840 --> 00:03:00.010
that govern the election for president. That
is what the United States Constitution says

00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:04.121
now.
Speaker 1: That's that's that's absurd. Think

00:03:04.121 --> 00:03:10.340
about even what Steve Scalise says their states
didn't follow their own rules, which is a

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:16.240
matter of the U.S. Constitution. No, it's
not. It's quite literally a matter of the

00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:21.570
state rules. It's not a U.S. constitutional
issue. This is backwards and sideways and

00:03:21.570 --> 00:03:28.180
nonsense. Now the other issue is if states
didn't follow their own rules, would that

00:03:28.180 --> 00:03:34.160
be a reason to give that state to Trump? Is
that evidence that Trump actually won a state

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:39.180
in which the rules weren't followed? So first,
you have to prove that then you have to prove

00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:43.050
it's materially substantive, then you have
to prove that it would have made the election

00:03:43.050 --> 00:03:48.520
go a different way if there were people who
voted absentee and then moved and then didn't

00:03:48.520 --> 00:03:53.900
call and say, I no longer live in the state.
That may be something that took place. It

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:59.070
may not. But then to say, and that means Trump
should have won the state is not backed by

00:03:59.070 --> 00:04:02.020
the evidence.
Speaker 2: You don't say that the states determine

00:04:02.020 --> 00:04:06.201
what the rules are. They say the state legislatures
determine that the states all services, they

00:04:06.201 --> 00:04:11.220
didn't follow those state legislative districts
and certify those legislative rules. Right.

00:04:11.220 --> 00:04:15.760
But at the end of the day, are we going to
follow what the Constitution says or not?

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:17.850
I hope we get back to what the Constitution
says.

00:04:17.850 --> 00:04:22.190
Speaker 1: Remember, this is not about the
U.S., it's just it just is not about the U.S.

00:04:22.190 --> 00:04:24.260
Constitution. He's talking about state rules,
state certification.

00:04:24.260 --> 00:04:28.590
Speaker 2: But clearly in a number of states,
they didn't follow those legislatively. So

00:04:28.590 --> 00:04:34.060
you think the election was stolen? But what
I said is there are states that didn't follow

00:04:34.060 --> 00:04:38.000
their legislatively set rules. That's what
the United States Constitution says. And so

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:41.440
then I think there are a lot of people that
want us to get back to what the Constitution

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:45.570
says we should be doing, not just with elections,
with a lot of other things.

00:04:45.570 --> 00:04:52.190
Speaker 1: This is where Trumpists end up
repeating. They ignored the Constitution in

00:04:52.190 --> 00:04:55.840
the 2020 election because they hear people
like Steve Scalise say it without explaining

00:04:55.840 --> 00:04:58.611
it. And they do.
Speaker 2: Yeah, I don't know. And then there

00:04:58.611 --> 00:05:02.460
are some people that want to just ignore what
the Constitution says and do their own thing.

00:05:02.460 --> 00:05:05.680
You know, that's been a debate that's been
going on in this country for a long time.

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:10.510
But just getting back to the Constitution,
I have that problem. And there certainly I

00:05:10.510 --> 00:05:12.430
can
Speaker 1: understand that getting back to

00:05:12.430 --> 00:05:16.420
the Constitution is different than did Trump
have the election stolen from him? And was

00:05:16.420 --> 00:05:20.040
he the rightful winner? Two totally different
things out there.

00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:23.490
Speaker 2: There was a rally for President
Trump yesterday, and a number of people said

00:05:23.490 --> 00:05:30.060
Joe Biden is backed by President Donald Trump
is my president. I guess the question is,

00:05:30.060 --> 00:05:34.610
do you think the election now last time? I
promise. Do you think the election was stolen

00:05:34.610 --> 00:05:37.900
or not? I understand you think there were
irregularities and things that need to be

00:05:37.900 --> 00:05:42.460
fixed. Do you think the election was stolen?
And it's not just a regulated states that

00:05:42.460 --> 00:05:46.680
did not follow the laws, which the Constitution?
Speaker 1: I think he'll answer this time.

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:48.740
What do you think
Speaker 2: says they're supposed to follow

00:05:48.740 --> 00:05:52.970
when you see states like Georgia cleaning
up some of the mess and people calling that

00:05:52.970 --> 00:05:58.090
Jim Crow law? That's a flat out lie. I think
with absolutely no law against that sort of

00:05:58.090 --> 00:06:00.410
thing. All right. Yeah. But but the law says,
again,

00:06:00.410 --> 00:06:03.440
Speaker 1: guys, he's not going to answer
the question. He's not going to answer the

00:06:03.440 --> 00:06:10.540
question. This is how you play coy and avoid
the wrath of Trump supporters and avoid the

00:06:10.540 --> 00:06:16.780
